/* Lesson quizzes — batch 16 */
window.LQ=window.LQ||{};
Object.assign(window.LQ,{
"p44m1":[
 {id:"p44m1q1", q:"A crew app's requirements say only: \"The app must be easy to use.\" Which rewrite turns that into a usability requirement in the ISO 9241-11 sense?", options:["Gloved splicers in a truck cab at dusk: 90% log a reel unaided, median under 90 s, SUS 75 or more","The app must feel intuitive and modern to everyone, on every device and in every setting where the company works","Every screen must pass a senior designer's review against the brand guide before each release","Users must give the app at least four stars in the app store within six months of launch"], answer:0, why:"ISO 9241-11 names specified users, goals and context, then measures effectiveness, efficiency and satisfaction; the crew-specific rewrite does all of that. \"Intuitive for everyone\" is the tempting wording, but it names no users or context and nothing you could test."},
 {id:"p44m1q2", q:"60,000 shoppers a month reach a checkout. A confusing address field makes 2% of them abandon, and the average order is $35. Roughly what does the defect cost per month?", options:["$4,200","$1,200","$42,000","$2.1 million"], answer:2, why:"60,000 × 0.02 = 1,200 lost buyers, and 1,200 × $35 = $42,000 a month. $4,200 uses 0.2% instead of 2%, $1,200 is the number of people rather than dollars, and $2.1 million is all checkout revenue, not the share lost."},
 {id:"p44m1q3", q:"An admin console lists \"Test failover\" and \"Run failover\" side by side, and both open the same \"Are you sure?\" dialog. An operator triggers a real failover by mistake. Which response matches the lesson's reading of the 2018 Hawaii alert?", options:["Discipline the operator and add a reminder to the runbook, since the software worked as it was designed to","Add a second, identical confirmation dialog to both items so every operator must click through twice","Make test and live visibly different, require a second person for live runs, and pre-build a rollback","Rename both menu items in plainer English but keep the shared dialog and approval path unchanged"], answer:2, why:"Hawaii's fixes treated it as a design and process defect: visibly different templates, a two-person rule and a prepared retraction. Blaming the operator is tempting but leaves near-identical choices in place, and a second identical dialog only trains people to click through twice."},
 {id:"p44m1q4", q:"A team runs a two-day design-thinking workshop, fills a wall with 300 sticky notes and a mood board, then starts coding. By the lesson's engineering rule, what is missing?", options:["More divergent ideas, because 300 notes is too few to converge on one strong concept","A visual design pass, because the first stage of UX work is making the screens look polished and on-brand","Nothing; design thinking is a one-time kickoff that is meant to come before any building","A falsifiable artifact: an evidenced problem statement, then a prototype with test results"], answer:3, why:"Each diamond must end in something that could prove you wrong: a problem statement backed by research and a prototype with test results. Without those the workshop is theatre. More ideas only widens the diamond again and still converges on nothing tested."},
 {id:"p44m1q5", q:"Staff keep approving MFA push prompts they did not start, and an attacker exploits this by sending prompt after prompt. Which change is the usable-security fix the lesson cites?", options:["Number matching: the prompt asks for a code shown on the login screen","Sending practice prompts more often so staff learn to tell real ones from fake ones","A longer warning paragraph in each prompt explaining how MFA-fatigue attacks work","An annual signed policy in which staff promise never to approve a prompt they did not start"], answer:0, why:"Push prompts trained people to tap Approve, which MFA-fatigue attacks such as the 2022 Uber breach exploited. Number matching changes the interaction so approval needs information only the person logging in has. More warning text or a signed policy leaves the habit-forming design in place."}
],
"p44m2":[
 {id:"p44m2q1", q:"You need to know how many of 900 technicians actually use the reel-scan button each week, not how many say they do. Which method fits best?", options:["A survey of all 900 asking how often they use the scan button","Six contextual inquiries at job sites, watching the scan step","Interviews asking whether they would use a faster scanner","Analytics on scan-button events across all accounts"], answer:3, why:"The question is behavioral and quantitative, which is analytics. A survey is tempting because it reaches everyone, but it records what people say, and people report their own behavior poorly. Contextual inquiry watches behavior, but six visits cannot count prevalence across 900 people."},
 {id:"p44m2q2", q:"A survey reaches 225 technicians. Using the worst case p = 0.5, what is the 95% margin of error, and what does it cover?", options:["About ±6.5 points, covering random sampling error only","About ±3.3 points, covering sampling and non-response error","About ±6.5 points, covering all error, including who chose to answer","About ±13 points, covering random sampling error only"], answer:0, why:"1.96 × √(0.25 ÷ 225) = 1.96 × 0.033 ≈ 0.065, so ±6.5 points. The tempting option has the right number but says it covers who answered; the margin covers random sampling error only, and a biased sample stays biased at any size."},
 {id:"p44m2q3", q:"You are interviewing a splicer about where footage errors come from. Which prompt is least likely to produce a misleading answer?", options:["Would you use voice entry for footage if we built it for you?","Tell me about the last time you logged a reel. What happened?","Don't you find the paper as-built sheets frustrating to fill in?","How likely are you to recommend our new logging app to a colleague?"], answer:1, why:"Asking about a specific past episode gets real behavior. The voice-entry question is tempting because it sounds open, but it is a hypothetical about future behavior, which people predict poorly, and it pitches your idea. The paper-sheet question leads the witness."},
 {id:"p44m2q4", q:"A sign-up funnel shows 40,000 visits → 8,000 open the form → 6,000 reach payment → 4,800 complete. Where is the biggest leak, and what should you do next?", options:["Payment to complete (80% continue); it is the last step, so it is the one to fix first","Form to payment (75% continue); the analytics already show why people leave there","Visit to form (20% continue); watch sessions or run a usability test to learn why","Visit to form (20% continue); the funnel data itself explains why people leave"], answer:2, why:"Step conversions are 8,000 ÷ 40,000 = 20%, then 75%, then 80%, so the first step leaks most. Analytics shows where and how many but never why, so pair the drop with observation or a test. Believing the funnel explains its own cause is the trap."},
 {id:"p44m2q5", q:"An in-house research plan: sessions are videoed, clips go into a company-wide demo, managers get a list of who took part, and anyone who stops early forfeits the gift card. What does the lesson require?", options:["Get approval from an ethics board (IRB) first; after that, the recording and reporting plan can stand as written","Separate consent for clips, no reporting to managers, and the incentive kept if someone stops","Nothing, because industry research is exempt from the Belmont principles that bind academic studies","Blur faces in the demo clips; the other parts of the plan are standard industry practice"], answer:1, why:"Respect for persons means voluntary, informed consent: separate consent for video and clips, participation never reported to managers, and the incentive kept if someone stops. Industry work needs no IRB, but the principles still bind it, so an IRB stamp is neither required nor enough."}
],
"p44m3":[
 {id:"p44m3q1", q:"A Sync button sits 280 px from where the thumb rests and is 20 px wide. With Fitts's law (Shannon form) and b = 0.15 s/bit, what does widening it to 40 px do?", options:["From 3.91 to 3.00 bits, saving about 0.14 s per tap","From 3.81 to 2.81 bits, saving about 0.15 s per tap","From 3.91 to 1.95 bits, halving the time of every tap","From 14 to 7 bits, saving about 1 s on every tap"], answer:0, why:"ID = log2(280/20 + 1) = log2(15) = 3.91 bits; at 40 px it is log2(8) = 3.00 bits, and 0.91 bits × 0.15 s ≈ 0.14 s. Dropping the +1 gives 3.81 and 2.81; doubling width removes about one bit, not half the time."},
 {id:"p44m3q2", q:"A technician deletes a splice record because she believed it was a duplicate; it was not. Which classification and fix fit Norman's model?", options:["A slip; put more space between the Delete and Save buttons","A mistake; show both records side by side and state what deleting removes","A slip; enlarge the Delete button so it is easier to hit accurately","A mistake; move the Delete button to the far corner of the screen"], answer:1, why:"She had the wrong goal, not the wrong action, so it is a mistake, fixed with a better conceptual model and confirmations that state consequences. Spacing and placement are slip fixes, for when the goal is right but the finger lands on the wrong control."},
 {id:"p44m3q3", q:"A picker lists 15 familiar, equally likely splice types. A designer proposes 3 categories of 5 instead. Using Hick's law in bits, which is right?", options:["Split: about 2.58 bits, since each menu has only five choices, so it is faster","Flat: 15 time units, split: 8, since time grows with the number of options","Flat: 4.00 bits; split: about 4.58 bits plus a second movement, so keep it flat","Both cost the same, because the total number of options does not change"], answer:2, why:"Flat: log2(15 + 1) = 4.00 bits. Split: log2(3 + 1) + log2(5 + 1) = 2.00 + 2.58 = 4.58 bits, plus a second movement. Counting only the second menu's 2.58 bits is the trap; linear growth applies to unfamiliar lists, not familiar ones."},
 {id:"p44m3q4", q:"A card-number field rejects spaces and turns red with \"Invalid\" from the first digit typed. Which redesign follows the lesson?", options:["Keep validating on every keystroke, but use a brighter red so the error is much harder to miss","Drop client-side checks and show one generic banner at the top when the server rejects the form","Accept spaces, validate on leaving the field, re-check live once an error shows, and recheck on the server","Put the format rule in the placeholder text and keep rejecting any spaces that the user types in"], answer:2, why:"Accept the formats people type, validate when a person leaves a field, re-check live once an error is showing, and always validate again on the server. Keystroke validation flags errors before the user has finished; a placeholder disappears on input and is no label."},
 {id:"p44m3q5", q:"Forty components hard-code #00e5ff. The brand cyan is about to change, and a dark mode is planned. Which handoff structure makes both a small change?", options:["Annotated PNG exports listing every hex value beside each component that uses it","A global search-and-replace of the hex value across the codebase before each release","Primitive tokens aliased by semantic ones (color-action), compiled to CSS custom properties","A separate Figma variant of every component for each color the brand might use later"], answer:3, why:"Tiered tokens store the decision once: cyan-400 holds the hex, color-action points to it, and modes swap values for dark mode; Style Dictionary compiles them to CSS variables. Search-and-replace looks quicker but must be repeated and cannot express light and dark pairings."}
],
"p44m4":[
 {id:"p44m4q1", q:"A gray text color has relative luminance 0.25 and sits on white (L = 1.0). What is the contrast ratio, and where may it be used under WCAG AA?", options:["4.0:1; it fails AA at every text size","3.5:1; fine for 24 px headings, not 16 px body text","3.5:1; passes AA for all text because it exceeds 3:1","5.25:1; passes AA for normal-size body text"], answer:1, why:"(1.0 + 0.05) ÷ (0.25 + 0.05) = 1.05 ÷ 0.30 = 3.5:1. Large text (24 px, or 18.66 px bold) needs 3:1; normal text needs 4.5:1. Dropping the 0.05 terms gives 4.0:1, and 3:1 is only the large-text and non-text threshold."},
 {id:"p44m4q2", q:"A lecture column set in 16 px Inter stretches to about 120 characters per line on a wide monitor, and readers lose their place. Which fix does the lesson recommend?", options:["Raise the line height to 2.5 so the eye can track the very long lines","Cap the column's width in CSS at a max-width of about 66ch","Switch to a condensed typeface so each long line is quicker to scan","Justify the text so both edges form a clean, even column"], answer:1, why:"45–75 characters, about 66, keeps the eye's return sweep reliable, set with max-width in ch. Extra line height is tempting and helps a little, but it leaves 120-character lines; justification adds uneven word gaps and a condensed face packs in even more characters."},
 {id:"p44m4q3", q:"A team builds dark mode by inverting the light palette: #000000 background, #ffffff text, the same saturated accents, and shadows for elevation. Which review comment is right?", options:["It is ideal: 21:1 is the highest contrast possible, so it is the most readable choice","Only the shadows need changing; colors that passed on white will also pass on black","Nothing needs review, since WCAG contrast rules apply only to light themes","Use about #121212 and off-white text, desaturate accents, lift surfaces, recheck ratios"], answer:3, why:"Pure white on black reaches 21:1 but causes halation, especially with astigmatism. Dark mode is a second design: dark gray surfaces, desaturated accents, elevation by lighter surfaces, and every ratio rechecked, because mid-grays that passed on white often fail on black."},
 {id:"p44m4q4", q:"A link-status dashboard shows a red dot for a failing span and a green dot for a passing one, with nothing else. Which change satisfies WCAG 1.4.1 Use of Color?", options:["Make the red more saturated and the green lighter so the two dots differ more","Pair each dot with a text label or a distinct shape, such as a check and a cross","Switch to a colorblind-safe Okabe–Ito pair so that hue alone can carry the status safely","Raise both dots' contrast against the page to 4.5:1 so that everyone can see them"], answer:1, why:"1.4.1 forbids color as the only way to convey meaning, so add text, icons or shape. A colorblind-safe palette is the tempting answer and helps charts, but meaning would still ride on hue alone; contrast is a separate criterion and does not tell red from green."},
 {id:"p44m4q5", q:"A home page has a carousel that auto-advances every 8 seconds indefinitely, with a parallax zoom on each slide. Which change set meets WCAG?", options:["Add a pause or stop control, and turn the parallax off under prefers-reduced-motion","Shorten each slide to 4 seconds so that no single slide moves for more than five seconds","Slow every transition to 1,000 ms with ease-in so the motion feels calmer to users","Add aria-live=\"assertive\" so screen readers announce each new slide as it appears"], answer:0, why:"Content that moves on its own for more than five seconds needs pause, stop or hide (2.2.2, Level A), and parallax can trigger vestibular symptoms, so honor prefers-reduced-motion. Shorter slides do not help: the carousel as a whole still moves indefinitely."}
],
"p44m5":[
 {id:"p44m5q1", q:"A button has aria-labelledby=\"t1\" (the element #t1 reads \"Delete reel\"), aria-label=\"Remove\", and visible text \"X\". What accessible name does a screen reader announce?", options:["Remove","X","Delete reel","Delete reel Remove X"], answer:2, why:"The accessible name comes first from aria-labelledby, then aria-label, then native labeling, then content, so #t1's text wins. \"Remove\" is tempting because aria-label overrides content, but aria-labelledby outranks aria-label."},
 {id:"p44m5q2", q:"A filter chip is <div class=\"chip on\" onclick=\"filter()\">Open</div>, and its active state is shown only by color. Which fix is best?", options:["Add role=\"button\" to the div and keep its existing onclick handler unchanged","<button type=\"button\" aria-pressed=\"true\">Open</button> plus an on/off icon","Add tabindex=\"5\" so the chip comes first in the keyboard Tab order","Add aria-label=\"Open filter\" to the div so that screen readers can announce a name"], answer:1, why:"A native button is focusable and fires on Enter and Space for free; aria-pressed exposes the state (4.1.2) and an icon removes color-only meaning (1.4.1). role=\"button\" changes what is announced but adds no keyboard behavior, and a positive tabindex breaks focus order."},
 {id:"p44m5q3", q:"In a single-page app, clicking \"Next lesson\" swaps the content, but focus stays on a button that no longer exists and screen-reader users hear nothing. What should each route change do?", options:["Give the first link of every view tabindex=\"1\" so that focus jumps straight there","Move focus to the new view's heading and update the document title","Read the whole new page aloud through an aria-live=\"assertive\" region","Leave focus alone, since screen readers re-read changed pages automatically"], answer:1, why:"On every route change, move focus to the new view's heading and update the title. A positive tabindex is tempting but breaks reading-order focus (2.4.3), and pouring a whole page into an assertive live region floods the listener."},
 {id:"p44m5q4", q:"A training video has unedited auto-captions full of errors, and its wiring diagrams are never described in the audio. What does WCAG 2.2 AA require?", options:["Nothing more, since automatic captions satisfy 1.2.2 once they are switched on","Edited, synchronized captions and audio description of the diagrams","A transcript only, because a transcript replaces both captions and description","Captions only, because audio description is required at Level AAA only"], answer:1, why:"Prerecorded video needs captions (Level A) and audio description of important visual content (AA). Auto-captions are a draft, and uncorrected errors fail the criterion. A transcript is the requirement for audio-only content and does not replace captions on video."},
 {id:"p44m5q5", q:"A US online bookstore with 40 staff and €8 million turnover sells e-books to consumers in Germany. As of October 2026, does the European Accessibility Act apply to it?", options:["No; the Act binds only companies that are based inside the EU","No; it does not apply to anyone until 28 June 2030","No; e-books fall only under EN 301 549 for public bodies","Yes; it covers e-books sold to EU consumers, wherever the seller is"], answer:3, why:"Since 28 June 2025 the EAA has applied to new products and services offered to EU consumers wherever the seller is based, and e-books and e-commerce are covered. The microenterprise exemption needs fewer than 10 staff; 2030 is only the transition for existing contracts and products."}
],
"p44m6":[
 {id:"p44m6q1", q:"A participant's SUS responses for items 1–10 are 3, 2, 4, 3, 4, 2, 3, 3, 4, 2. What is the score, and how should you read it?", options:["75, above the average of about 68","50, in the bottom sixth of products","65, just below the average of about 68","65%, meaning about two-thirds of it is usable"], answer:2, why:"Odd items 3,4,4,3,4 score response − 1 = 13; even items 2,3,2,3,2 score 5 − response = 13. (13 + 13) × 2.5 = 65, just under the ~68 average. 75 comes from multiplying the raw sum by 2.5, and SUS is never a percentage."},
 {id:"p44m6q2", q:"A usability problem affects 20% of one user group. What is the chance that a five-user test round shows it at least once?", options:["About 33%","About 67%","About 84%","100%, since 5 × 20% = 100%"], answer:1, why:"1 − (1 − 0.20)^5 = 1 − 0.33 ≈ 67%. 84% is the figure for problems that affect 31% of users, 33% is the chance of missing it entirely, and probabilities do not simply add across users."},
 {id:"p44m6q3", q:"Baseline completion is 20%, and you want to detect a 4-point lift with 80% power at α = 0.05. By Lehr's rule, how many users do you need per arm?", options:["About 64 per arm","About 2,000 per arm","About 800 per arm","About 1,600 per arm"], answer:3, why:"n ≈ 16 × p(1 − p) ÷ δ² = 16 × 0.16 ÷ 0.0016 = 1,600 per arm. Forgetting to square δ gives 64, and using p instead of p(1 − p) gives 2,000. A product without that traffic is better served by a five-person test."},
 {id:"p44m6q4", q:"A sync spinner shows no progress during a 40-second upload, so crews tap Sync again and create duplicate records. Which of Nielsen's heuristics is most directly violated?", options:["Visibility of system status","Recognition rather than recall","Aesthetic and minimalist design","Help and documentation"], answer:0, why:"Users cannot tell the system is still working, so they repeat the action; past about 10 seconds the lesson calls for percent-done progress. Error prevention also bears on the duplicates, but the root cause is missing status, and documentation would not reach users mid-task."},
 {id:"p44m6q5", q:"In a five-user test, 4 of 5 participants complete the task. A manager wants the report to say \"80% task success.\" What is the honest report?", options:["80% success, accurate to within about ±5 points because every session was observed","4 of 5 succeeded; the true rate's 95% interval runs from about 36% to 98%","80% success, which beats the 78% target, so the design is ready to ship as it is","Nothing; five-user tests produce no findings that are worth reporting to anyone"], answer:1, why:"With 4 of 5, the adjusted-Wald 95% interval runs from about 36% to 98%. Five users find problems; they do not measure rates. \"80% ±5\" is the tempting error, because watching every session says nothing about the population rate."}
],
"p44m7":[
 {id:"p44m7q1", q:"A draft portfolio case study shows 14 polished screens, lists 11 research methods, and ends: \"Users loved it.\" What should change first?", options:["Add more screens, since hiring managers judge visual polish before anything else","Add problem evidence, key decisions with rejected options, and measured outcomes","List even more methods, so a hiring panel can see the full range of what the designer knows","Cut the reflection section, since admitting what went wrong looks weak to a hiring panel"], answer:1, why:"A case study is an argument: problem with evidence, methods chosen for a reason, key decisions with trade-offs, measured outcomes and reflection. More screens is tempting, but managers skim for minutes looking for framing and results; screens only illustrate the story."},
 {id:"p44m7q2", q:"A confirmation dialog asks whether to delete a reel record. Which pair of button labels follows the lesson's microcopy rules?", options:["OK / Cancel","Yes / No","Delete reel / Keep reel","Proceed / Abort operation"], answer:2, why:"Buttons should be verbs that say what happens, so a person can act without rereading the question. \"OK / Cancel\" is the familiar default, but OK does not say whether it deletes, and \"Abort operation\" is jargon that sounds alarming."},
 {id:"p44m7q3", q:"In a design review of a crew app's sync screen, which comment is the most useful critique?", options:["I like it overall; it feels clean and modern, and I think the crews will probably love it too","Make the Sync button blue, because blue buttons always test better than any other color","Crews call uploading \"push,\" so \"Sync\" may be misread; does that threaten same-day logging?","Replace this layout with the one our biggest competitor's app uses for the same screen"], answer:2, why:"Useful critique ties an observation and its reason to the goal. \"I like it\" is not feedback, and \"make it blue\" is direction rather than critique: it prescribes a solution without naming the problem it solves or checking it against the goal."},
 {id:"p44m7q4", q:"A tester has six months of accessibility work and wants IAAP credentials. Which plan matches the lesson?", options:["Sit CPACC first, then WAS after about a year of remediation work","Sit WAS first, since it is the technical exam and CPACC adds nothing","Sit both in one session, because the CPWA requires them taken together","Skip CPACC, since the Google certificate substitutes for it toward CPWA"], answer:0, why:"CPACC is broad and foundational and lets candidates with less than a year's experience describe their background; WAS is the technical exam, screened for hands-on work. Passing both earns the CPWA, with no same-session rule. Taking WAS first skips the foundation."},
 {id:"p44m7q5", q:"A career changer in a mid-size regional market reads that UX's median entry-level salary is $125,000 (Lightcast job postings, 2025). How should he set his expectation?", options:["Expect about $125,000, since a median is what a typical first offer pays","Treat it as a ceiling; check BLS metro data and local postings, and expect less","Expect at least $98,000, since the BLS national median sets the floor","Ignore pay data entirely, since UX salaries cannot be estimated in advance"], answer:1, why:"Posting data skews toward big-metro and big-tech roles, so the lesson calls it a ceiling. A career changer's first offer usually sits below it, and a regional market below the national BLS median near $98,000. Reading the posting median as a typical offer is the trap."}
]
});
Object.assign(window.LQ,{
"p45m1":[
 {id:"p45m1q1", q:"An ISP build passes 300 homes, signs 120 subscribers at $75 a month, and finishes on its planned date. In the output → outcome → benefit chain, which item is the outcome?", options:["120 subscribers signed","300 homes passed","$108,000 a year of revenue","Finishing on the planned date"], answer:0, why:"Homes passed is the output, what the project built; subscribers are the outcome, the change the output enables; revenue and families online are the benefit. Finishing on time is a triangle measure, not a link in the value chain."},
 {id:"p45m1q2", q:"Two neighboring builds each pass 300 homes and sell service at $75 a month. A finished on time and on budget at $360,000 with a 20% take rate. B finished three weeks late at $378,000 with a 45% take rate. Which pays back faster?", options:["A, because it met scope, schedule and cost, the three sides of the triangle","A, at about 3.1 years against B's 6.7, since B overran its budget","Neither can be judged, because payback is undefined once a job overruns","B, at about 3.1 years against A's 6.7, because benefits drive payback"], answer:3, why:"A: 60 subscribers × $900 = $54,000 a year, so 360,000 ÷ 54,000 ≈ 6.7 years. B: 135 × $900 = $121,500, so 378,000 ÷ 121,500 ≈ 3.1 years. Judging by the triangle alone is the trap; benefits are what justified the build."},
 {id:"p45m1q3", q:"A job has two parts: a directional bore under a state highway, whose route the permit fixes, and a customer activation portal whose requirements are still unclear. Which life cycle fits?", options:["Hybrid: plan the bore predictively, build the portal in sprints, sync at milestones","Fully adaptive for both, since the portal's unclear requirements dominate the risk","Fully predictive: freeze the portal's specification now, along with the bore route","Incremental only: hand the bore over to the customer one finished foot at a time"], answer:0, why:"The permit fixes the bore and change is expensive, so it suits a predictive plan; the portal's requirements will emerge, so it suits sprints. Running both, synchronized at milestones, is hybrid. Freezing the portal spec early is tempting but locks in guesses."},
 {id:"p45m1q4", q:"A vendor says: \"The average IT project overruns by 27%, so adding 27% to our $2 million budget covers us.\" What does the lesson's data say about that plan?", options:["It is sound, because the mean overrun already includes both typical and worst cases","It is excessive, since Standish found 31% succeed, so no buffer is needed at all","It should be 45%, which is McKinsey's overrun figure for IT projects of every size","The mean hides a fat tail: one IT project in six ran about 200% over budget"], answer:3, why:"Flyvbjerg and Budzier found a 27% average, but one project in six was a black swan averaging 200% over cost, so the mean hides the real risk. The 45% figure is McKinsey's for projects over $15 million, not for all IT projects."},
 {id:"p45m1q5", q:"A RACI row for \"Splice and test feeder\" reads: splicing lead R, construction manager A, OSP engineer A, owner's inspector C, ISP customer team I. What is wrong with it?", options:["The customer team should be Consulted on every deliverable, never only Informed","The splicing lead should be Accountable too, since that lead does the actual work","It has two Accountables, but each deliverable needs exactly one A","The inspector should be Responsible, since the inspector signs off on the work"], answer:2, why:"Accountable is the single person who answers for the deliverable, so each row has exactly one A; with two, each can assume the other owns it. Making the doer Accountable as well is tempting, but Responsible already captures who does the work."}
],
"p45m2":[
 {id:"p45m2q1", q:"Two activities run in sequence. X: O = 4, M = 6, P = 14 days. Y: O = 8, M = 10, P = 18 days. What are the path's PERT mean and standard deviation?", options:["16.0 days, σ ≈ 1.7","20.0 days, σ ≈ 2.4","18.0 days, σ ≈ 2.4","18.0 days, σ ≈ 3.3"], answer:2, why:"PERT means: (4 + 24 + 14) ÷ 6 = 7 and (8 + 40 + 18) ÷ 6 = 11, so 18 days. Each σ = (P − O) ÷ 6 ≈ 1.67; variances add, so σ = √(2.78 + 2.78) ≈ 2.36. Adding the σs (3.3) is the trap; 20 is the triangular mean."},
 {id:"p45m2q2", q:"Network: A 5 d (start); B 12 d and C 4 d, both after A; D 6 d after B and C; E 9 d after A; F 3 d after D and E. If extra rigs keep shortening B, when do they stop shortening the project?", options:["At B = 4 days, when A–C–D–F also becomes critical at 18 days","When B reaches 0 days; the finish is then 14 days, set by A–D–F alone","When B reaches 9 days; past that point, A–E–F becomes the critical path","Never; every day cut from B always comes straight off the finish date"], answer:0, why:"Paths: A–B–D–F = 26, A–C–D–F = 18, A–E–F = 17. Cutting B shortens the job until A–B–D–F falls to 18 at B = 4; then A–C–D–F is critical too and more rigs buy nothing. A–E–F, at 17 days, is never the limit."},
 {id:"p45m2q3", q:"The pole owner must approve make-ready on 40 poles but has little interest in your subdivision. Residents along the bore route care a lot but cannot stop the job. Which power-interest strategies fit?", options:["Pole owner: monitor; residents: manage closely","Pole owner: manage closely; residents: monitor","Pole owner: keep informed; residents: keep satisfied","Pole owner: keep satisfied; residents: keep informed"], answer:3, why:"High power and low interest is keep satisfied: meet its requirements early so it never blocks you. Low power and high interest is keep informed, such as notices before the rig arrives. Monitoring the pole owner is tempting because it seems uninterested, but its approval can stop the job."},
 {id:"p45m2q4", q:"A portal WBS has 1 Project management, 2 Requirements and design, 3 Build, 4 Test. The team must also deploy and hand over, and 3 Build includes \"Buy chairs for the team room.\" What is the right critique?", options:["It is fine; a WBS lists activities in order, and any gaps get filled in the schedule","It needs a start and finish date on every element, since a WBS is the calendar","Only the chairs are wrong; the hand-over can be added later through change control","It breaks the 100% rule: hand-over is missing and the chairs are outside scope"], answer:3, why:"The 100% rule says the children hold all of the parent's work and nothing else, so a missing deploy-and-hand-over element and an out-of-scope purchase both break it. Treating the WBS as an activity list is the misconception; activities and their sequence come later."},
 {id:"p45m2q5", q:"Your bore estimate is 30 days. The company's past bores finished at a median of 1.10 and an 80th percentile of 1.40 times their estimates. What should the charter commit, and where do the extra days go?", options:["30 days planned, 42 committed at P80, with the 12 days held as visible contingency","Quietly pad the crew's estimate to 42 days so the slack is built in where nobody sees it","Commit 33 days, the historical median, since the median is the most likely single result","Commit 30 days, since other jobs' history says nothing about this particular bore"], answer:0, why:"Reference-class forecasting gives P80 = 30 × 1.40 = 42 days. Hidden padding gets spent (Parkinson's law) and corrupts your history, so plan honestly and hold the risk in an explicit contingency reserve. The median is only a 50% commitment."}
],
"p45m3":[
 {id:"p45m3q1", q:"A bore is budgeted at $12 a foot. By Friday the plan called for 4,000 ft. The reconciled as-builts show 4,500 ft, and costs to date are $59,400. What is the status?", options:["Behind schedule but under cost: SPI 0.89, CPI 1.10","Ahead of schedule and under cost: SPI 1.13, CPI 1.10","Over budget by $11,400, since AC exceeds PV by that amount","Ahead of schedule but over cost: SPI 1.13, CPI 0.91"], answer:3, why:"PV = 4,000 × $12 = $48,000; EV = 4,500 × $12 = $54,000; AC = $59,400. SPI = 54,000 ÷ 48,000 = 1.13 and CPI = 54,000 ÷ 59,400 = 0.91. Comparing AC with PV mixes cost and schedule; only EV shows what the money bought."},
 {id:"p45m3q2", q:"BAC is $300,000, EV $120,000 and AC $150,000. The overrun came from a single washout that is now repaired, and the remaining work should run at plan rates. Which EAC fits?", options:["$330,000","$375,000","$180,000","$300,000"], answer:0, why:"A one-time cause already behind you means the rest runs at budget: EAC = AC + (BAC − EV) = 150,000 + 180,000 = $330,000. BAC ÷ CPI = $375,000 assumes the 0.80 efficiency continues, the tempting default but wrong for a repaired one-off."},
 {id:"p45m3q3", q:"BAC is $200,000, EV $90,000 and AC $100,000. What is the TCPI, and what does it mean?", options:["0.91; the rest can run 9% less efficiently and still finish on budget","1.11; the job is forecast to finish 11% over its budget","1.10; the rest must run 10% more efficiently than planned","0.90; the job as a whole is running 10% over its budget"], answer:2, why:"TCPI = (BAC − EV) ÷ (BAC − AC) = 110,000 ÷ 100,000 = 1.10: the remaining $110,000 of work must be done for $100,000. 0.90 is the CPI to date and 1.11 its inverse; neither is the efficiency required from here."},
 {id:"p45m3q4", q:"A storm cuts a feeder in three places. The scope will not be clear until crews expose the damage, and service must be restored tonight. Which contract approach fits?", options:["Time and materials, capped with a not-to-exceed","Firm fixed price, so the seller carries all the cost risk","Unit price per splice, agreed once the damage is fully known","Cost plus fee with no cap, since the uncertainty is so high"], answer:0, why:"The lesson lists time and materials, capped with a not-to-exceed, for emergency restoration and unclear scope. Fixed price is tempting because it moves risk to the seller, but with undefined scope the seller prices in the risk and every unknown becomes a change order."},
 {id:"p45m3q5", q:"Rock forces the bore across the street. The foreman redlines the prints and keeps drilling, and nobody prices the change. What should have happened?", options:["Nothing yet; settle the extra cost with the owner at closeout using the redlined prints","Refuse the extra work, since any change from the prints counts as gold plating","Absorb the cost quietly, because change orders tend to upset the customer","Request, impact analysis, decision by the named authority, then a signed change order"], answer:3, why:"Integrated change control runs every change through request, impact analysis, a decision by the authority named in the plan, baseline updates and communication, with the change order signed before the work where possible. Settling at closeout is tempting but leaves the cost unapproved and disputed."}
],
"p45m4":[
 {id:"p45m4q1", q:"You logged the risk \"locates may be late, idling the crew.\" Today the locator did not show, and the crew is idle. What should happen to the item?", options:["Leave it in the risk register and raise its probability score to 5","Delete it from the register, since a risk that has happened is gone","Open it in the issue log with an owner and a due date, and work it now","Note it in lessons learned at closeout, where field problems belong"], answer:2, why:"A risk that has occurred is an issue: it goes to the issue log with an owner, priority and due date, and the daily meeting works it. Raising its probability is tempting, but the uncertainty is gone, and lessons are captured during the job, not only at closeout."},
 {id:"p45m4q2", q:"Segment choice: bore for $24,000 with a 25% chance of rock adding $16,000, or go aerial for $18,000 with a 60% chance of $20,000 in pole replacements. By expected value, which is cheaper?", options:["Aerial, because its $18,000 base price is $6,000 cheaper","Aerial: expected $26,000 against the bore's $28,000","Bore, since a threat with only a 25% probability can be ignored","Bore: expected $28,000 against aerial's $30,000"], answer:3, why:"Bore: 24,000 + 0.25 × 16,000 = $28,000. Aerial: 18,000 + 0.60 × 20,000 = $30,000. Comparing base prices ignores the risk, and $26,000 comes from using 40% instead of 60%. With values this close, schedule (make-ready delays) may still decide."},
 {id:"p45m4q3", q:"Three parallel paths each have an 80% chance of finishing on time, and all three must finish before one milestone. How likely is the milestone to be on time?", options:["80%, the same as each path","About 51%","About 99%","About 40%"], answer:1, why:"On-time probabilities multiply at a merge: 0.8 × 0.8 × 0.8 = 0.512. Assuming the milestone inherits each path's 80% ignores merge bias, and 99% is the chance that at least one path is on time, which is not what the milestone needs."},
 {id:"p45m4q4", q:"A bore pit will be 6 ft deep beside a road. The foreman's plan is hard hats and vests for everyone plus a \"be careful at the edge\" talk. What does the lesson say?", options:["That is enough, since PPE is the most effective control on the hierarchy","Add a second safety talk every afternoon, because administrative controls outrank engineering ones","Nothing more is needed, since protective systems are required only from 10 ft","Add a protective system such as a trench box, or keep the pit under 5 ft; PPE comes last"], answer:3, why:"The hierarchy runs elimination, substitution, engineering, administrative, then PPE. At 5 ft or deeper a protective system is generally required (29 CFR 1926 Subpart P), so a trench box or a shallower pit comes first. Talks and PPE are the weakest layers, not the plan."},
 {id:"p45m4q5", q:"A breach of a customer portal would cost $150,000 (SLE) and is expected once every 10 years. A control costing $9,000 a year would cut that to once every 50 years. On ALE alone, is it worth it?", options:["Yes: ALE falls from $15,000 to $3,000, saving $12,000 for $9,000","No: it costs $9,000 a year against the $3,000 ALE that remains","No: $9,000 a year exceeds the $1,500 ALE of a once-a-decade breach","Yes: it avoids the full $150,000 loss, which far exceeds $9,000"], answer:0, why:"ALE = SLE × ARO: 150,000 × 0.1 = $15,000 before and 150,000 × 0.02 = $3,000 after, a $12,000 reduction for $9,000. Comparing the cost with the remaining ALE is the trap, because the control buys the reduction; the full SLE ignores probability."}
],
"p45m5":[
 {id:"p45m5q1", q:"A support queue holds 24 tickets in progress and the team finishes 6 a week. They set a WIP limit of 12, and throughput stays at 6 a week. What happens to average cycle time?", options:["Nothing, since WIP limits change workload and not cycle time","From 0.25 weeks to 0.5 weeks, as fewer items share the work","From 4 weeks to 8 weeks, since less work is started each week","From 4 weeks to 2 weeks, with nobody working any harder"], answer:3, why:"Little's law: cycle time = WIP ÷ throughput, so 24 ÷ 6 = 4 weeks before and 12 ÷ 6 = 2 weeks after. Dividing throughput by WIP gives the 0.25 and 0.5 figures, with the units upside down. With throughput steady, less WIP means less waiting."},
 {id:"p45m5q2", q:"The last five Sprints delivered 30, 24, 27, 33 and 26 points. The remaining backlog is 198 points. What forecast does the lesson recommend?", options:["7.1 Sprints, using the mean velocity of 28 points","6.0 to 8.3 Sprints, so forecast six to nine","6.0 Sprints, using the team's best recent velocity","8.3 Sprints, since only the slowest Sprint is safe to quote"], answer:1, why:"Forecast with a range: 198 ÷ 33 = 6.0 to 198 ÷ 24 = 8.25 Sprints, so six to nine. The mean-based 7.1 looks precise but hides the spread, the tempting error; a single best- or worst-case number throws away the same information."},
 {id:"p45m5q3", q:"In a team's Daily Scrum, each developer reports yesterday's tasks to the engineering manager, who then assigns today's work. It runs 40 minutes. What does the 2020 Scrum Guide say?", options:["Make it the Developers' 15-minute event to inspect progress toward the Sprint Goal","It is fine as long as the Scrum Master attends and keeps notes on every update","Hold it weekly instead, since 40 minutes a day is too expensive for the team","It is fine, because reporting status upward is the purpose of the Daily Scrum"], answer:0, why:"The Daily Scrum is a 15-minute event for the Developers to inspect progress toward the Sprint Goal and adapt their own plan. A manager assigning work turns it into a status report, the classic anti-pattern, and a Scrum Master taking notes does not fix that."},
 {id:"p45m5q4", q:"Under the Last Planner System, foremen committed to 32 assignments this week. They completed 24 of them and also finished 4 unplanned jobs. What is percent plan complete?", options:["87.5%","133%","75%","25%"], answer:2, why:"PPC = assignments completed ÷ assignments planned = 24 ÷ 32 = 75%. Counting the 4 unplanned jobs (28 ÷ 32) is the trap: PPC measures how reliably commitments are kept, so work outside the plan does not raise it. 133% is the ratio inverted."},
 {id:"p45m5q5", q:"Three teams on one product keep blocking each other on a shared database layer. A consultant proposes SAFe with an Agile Release Train. What does the lesson advise first?", options:["Adopt SAFe, because quarterly PI Planning will remove the dependency problem","Measure each team's velocity and reward the fastest team to raise the pace","Split into more, smaller teams so each owns a thinner slice of the database","Remove the cross-team dependencies first, and scale the process last"], answer:3, why:"No scaling model fixes tangled architecture or cross-team dependencies, so remove those first and add process last. SAFe is tempting because PI Planning makes dependencies visible, but it coordinates around them rather than removing them, and rewarding velocity inflates points."}
],
"p45m6":[
 {id:"p45m6q1", q:"A tech who started on the OTDR three weeks ago knows the basics but is frustrated after misreading two traces and is losing confidence. Which situational-leadership style fits this task?", options:["Delegating: give the outcome and the deadline, then step back","Directing: show each step, then watch every trace closely","Coaching: keep giving direction, and explain the reasons behind it","Supporting: listen and encourage, but give no further direction"], answer:2, why:"Someone still learning and frustrated gets coaching: direction plus the reasons. Directing fits a new hire who has never done the task, supporting fits a capable person who is shaken, and delegating fits a veteran. The style follows development level on this task."},
 {id:"p45m6q2", q:"A crew member set up a lane closure only after the truck had already stopped in the lane. Which piece of feedback follows the SBI model?", options:["Yesterday on Oak Street you set the closure after the truck stopped; a car nearly hit the tailgate","You are always careless with the traffic setup, the whole crew has noticed it, and it has to stop now","Great job overall this week, but maybe watch the cones a bit more next time, okay?","Everyone on this crew needs to do a lot better on traffic control from now on"], answer:0, why:"SBI names the situation (Oak Street, yesterday), the specific behavior (closure set late) and its impact (a near miss), then opens a conversation. \"Always careless\" labels the person instead of the behavior, and vague group feedback or a softened aside tells no one what to change."},
 {id:"p45m6q3", q:"Two developers disagree on an API design. The decision will shape the product for years, they will work together long after, and there is a week before it is needed. Which conflict mode fits best?", options:["Compromise: split the difference between the designs so that both get part of what they want","Force: the PM picks a design today to end the argument","Avoid: let it cool off and revisit it if it comes back","Collaborate: work it together to find a design that meets both concerns"], answer:3, why:"Collaborate when both the issue and the relationship matter and there is time to work it. Compromise is tempting but suits time pressure and leaves both partly unsatisfied; force is for safety and ethics, and avoiding only sends the conflict underground."},
 {id:"p45m6q4", q:"A 1,000-ft rock segment costs you $25 a foot, your walk-away floor. The owner's alternative is a specialist at $30 a foot plus $5,000 to mobilize, plus $4,000 of revenue lost to the delay. What is the ZOPA?", options:["$25,000 to $39,000","$25,000 to $30,000","$30,000 to $39,000","$25,000 to $35,000"], answer:0, why:"Your floor is 1,000 × $25 = $25,000. The owner's walk-away is the full cost of its BATNA: 30,000 + 5,000 + 4,000 = $39,000, and the ZOPA lies between the two. Stopping at the specialist's $30,000 rate ignores mobilization and delay, the most common error."},
 {id:"p45m6q5", q:"The build is amber (SPI 0.88). Your boss asks you to report green this week to avoid worrying the sponsor, since you expect to recover. What does PMI's Code of Ethics point you to?", options:["Report green, since recovery is expected and amber would only cause worry","Report amber with the numbers, the cause and a recovery plan with options","Skip this week's report until it is clear whether the recovery will hold","Report green on the summary line and give the real SPI in a footnote"], answer:1, why:"Honesty is one of the Code's four values: never report green when it is amber. Bad news goes early, with options, so the sponsor can decide. A green headline with the true figure buried in a footnote is still a misleading report, and silence hides the problem."}
],
"p45m7":[
 {id:"p45m7q1", q:"For your home lab you need real dependency logic, float and a critical path on a 300-activity fiber schedule, with no budget for licenses. Which tool fits?", options:["Asana, using its timeline view and light task dependencies","Jira, using a Kanban board with one column per crew","A shared spreadsheet grid with a Gantt-style view added","ProjectLibre, a free open-source desktop scheduler"], answer:3, why:"Dependency logic and float need a true scheduling engine (Project, P6 or ProjectLibre), and ProjectLibre is the free one that opens Microsoft Project files. Asana is tempting for its timeline, but its dependencies are light, and Jira manages workflow rather than computing a critical path."},
 {id:"p45m7q2", q:"A PM's morning-meeting query, project = FTTH AND statusCategory = Done AND duedate < now() ORDER BY priority DESC, is meant to list overdue work. Why does it fail?", options:["It selects finished items; overdue work needs statusCategory != Done","now() is not valid in JQL, so the date comparison is silently skipped","ORDER BY must come before the filter clauses for Jira to accept it","Past-due work needs duedate > now(), since the due date comes later"], answer:0, why:"Overdue means not finished and past its due date, so the filter needs statusCategory != Done with duedate < now(). As written it lists completed work. Flipping the date comparison is the tempting fix, but duedate > now() would return items that are not yet due."},
 {id:"p45m7q3", q:"Dashboard thresholds are green at 0.95 or better, amber from 0.85 to 0.95, and red below 0.85. The build shows CPI 0.93 and SPI 0.83. What does the dashboard show?", options:["Both amber, since each index is within 0.15 of 1.0","Cost red and schedule amber","Cost amber and schedule red","Both amber, since their average of 0.88 sits in the amber band"], answer:2, why:"CPI 0.93 falls in the 0.85–0.95 band, so cost is amber; SPI 0.83 is below 0.85, so schedule is red. Averaging the two indexes is tempting, but each is judged against its own threshold, set before the job, so a red schedule is not hidden."},
 {id:"p45m7q4", q:"A new-city launch groups 12 serving-area builds, the portal and the marketing campaign, managed together for subscriber growth. Its PMO requires every build to use the PMO's schedule template and stage gates. Which pair is right?", options:["A portfolio, with a directive PMO","A program, with a controlling PMO","A program, with a supportive PMO","A portfolio, with a controlling PMO"], answer:1, why:"Related projects managed together for benefits none delivers alone form a program; a portfolio is everything the organization funds. A PMO that requires compliance is controlling. Supportive PMOs only offer templates and training, and directive ones manage the projects themselves."},
 {id:"p45m7q5", q:"A CAPM mock gives you 70% in Fundamentals (36% weight), 90% Predictive (17%), 90% Agile (20%) and 50% Business Analysis (27%). What is your weighted score, and what next?", options:["About 72%; review Business Analysis first, your weakest domain","About 75%; review Business Analysis first, your weakest domain","About 72%; drill Predictive and Agile, where you already score 90%","About 83%; book the exam, since three of four domains are strong"], answer:0, why:"0.36 × 70 + 0.17 × 90 + 0.20 × 90 + 0.27 × 50 = 25.2 + 15.3 + 18.0 + 13.5 = 72%. The unweighted mean is 75%, the tempting slip. Business Analysis is 27% of the exam, so the plan says weakest domain first."}
]
});
Object.assign(window.LQ,{
"p46m1":[
 {id:"p46m1q1", q:"A beginner cannot hold a 12-hex-digit MAC address in mind while typing it, but a network engineer reads it once and types it straight. What best explains the difference?", options:["The engineer's working-memory span has been trained up from about four items to twelve","The engineer holds it as a few meaningful chunks, within the limit of about four","The beginner is not paying attention, and attention alone raises the limit to seven","The engineer rehearses each digit faster, so none of the twelve has time to decay"], answer:1, why:"Working memory holds about four chunks (Cowan, 2001), and expertise works around the limit by chunking: the engineer sees a vendor prefix and a few familiar groups, not twelve symbols. Training does not enlarge the span itself, as Chase and Simon's random chess positions showed."},
 {id:"p46m1q2", q:"Your Security+ exam is ten weeks (70 days) away. By the Cepeda et al. rule of thumb, when should the first review of this week's material fall?", options:["About 7 to 14 days from now, then let the gaps expand","Tomorrow, and then again every single day right up until the exam","About five weeks from now, halfway between today and the exam","In the last week, so it is fresh on test day"], answer:0, why:"Place the first review at roughly 10–20 percent of the time until you need the knowledge: 7–14 days of 70, then let the gaps expand. Reviewing only in the last week is massed practice, and daily review retrieves items long before they are near forgetting."},
 {id:"p46m1q3", q:"In the Recall engine, a card's interval is 10 days and its ease is 2.5. You rate it Hard, then Good at the following review. What are the next two intervals?", options:["10 minutes, then 1 day, since Hard resets the card's history","25 days, then about 59 days","6 days, then 15 days, since the ease stays at 2.5","6 days, then about 14 days (6 × 2.36)"], answer:3, why:"Hard multiplies the interval by 0.6 (10 → 6 days) and lowers the ease by 0.14 (2.5 → 2.36); Good then gives 6 × 2.36 ≈ 14 days. Keeping the ease at 2.5 gives 15, the tempting slip, and only Again sends a card back in ten minutes."},
 {id:"p46m1q4", q:"You have 30 minutes for a new module. Which plan should give the best recall one week later?", options:["Read it three times, highlighting the key terms on each pass","Read it once, then write a detailed summary with the page open","Read it once, close it and recall it, then check and fix the gaps","Read it twice, then build an elaborate concept map straight from the text"], answer:2, why:"Retrieval with the page closed, then feedback, is the testing effect: Roediger and Karpicke found 61 versus 40 percent a week later, and Karpicke and Blunt found retrieval beat concept mapping. Rereading and open-book summaries feel fluent, which is the illusion of competence."},
 {id:"p46m1q5", q:"The night before a class that starts a new module, you consider an all-nighter to finish reviewing the previous one. Which choice best protects learning?", options:["Sleep; an all-nighter skips tonight's replay and cuts tomorrow's encoding","Stay up, since four extra hours of review outweigh one night of lost sleep","Stay up, then take a 20-minute nap before class to restore encoding fully","Sleep only after class, since sleep consolidates procedural skills alone"], answer:0, why:"Sleep replays the day's learning to the neocortex, and one sleepless night cut next-day hippocampal encoding by about 40 percent (Yoo et al., 2007), so the all-nighter loses both halves. Early-night slow-wave sleep favors declarative facts, so sleep is not only for skills."}
],
"p46m2":[
 {id:"p46m2q1", q:"A card's FSRS stability S is 20 days. With R(t) = 1 / (1 + t/(9S)), when is the next review if you set the desired retention to 0.80 instead of 0.90?", options:["After 20 days","After 45 days","After 16 days","After 25 days"], answer:1, why:"Solve 0.80 = 1 / (1 + t/180): 1 + t/180 = 1.25, so t = 45 days, which is 2.25S. At 0.90, t = S = 20 days. A lower target buys fewer reviews with more forgetting; scaling S by 0.8 or dividing it by 0.8 does not follow the curve."},
 {id:"p46m2q2", q:"You are learning 20 new port-number cards. Which plan follows the Rawson–Dunlosky 3×3 guideline?", options:["Recall each card correctly just once today, then leave them until the exam week","Read the full list three times on each of three nights in a row, without testing","Run all 20 cards nine times in a row in one long session today","Three correct recalls each today, then one in each of three later sessions"], answer:3, why:"Rawson and Dunlosky's rule is about three correct recalls in the first session, then relearning to one correct recall in roughly three further spaced sessions. Nine reps in one sitting is massed practice, and rereading the list three nights running skips retrieval altogether."},
 {id:"p46m2q3", q:"Practicing for an exam that mixes subnetting, ACL and routing questions, you score 90% on blocked sets but 65% on mixed sets. What should you do?", options:["Keep mixing; it feels worse but trains choosing which method applies","Switch to blocked sets, since the 90% shows that they work better","Block until each type is perfect, then keep the types separate","Mix only the topics you already score 90% or more on in blocked sets"], answer:0, why:"Interleaving trains discrimination, the \"which method applies?\" step a real exam demands; in Rohrer and Taylor's data it lost during practice yet won a week later. The higher blocked score is the tempting signal, but it measures performance during practice, not retained learning."},
 {id:"p46m2q4", q:"Which way of studying a TCP-handshake figure applies dual coding most effectively?", options:["Read the figure's caption aloud three times while looking at it","Color-highlight every label on the figure in a different color","Study it, close it, redraw it from memory, then compare and correct","Copy the figure neatly into your notes while it is open beside you"], answer:2, why:"Dual coding pairs words with a matching picture to create two retrieval routes, and redrawing from memory adds retrieval and feedback. Copying the figure with it open is tempting because it feels thorough, but it is transcription; highlighting labels adds color, not a route."},
 {id:"p46m2q5", q:"Explaining DNS resolution to ADA, you stall at how the resolver finds the authoritative server and reach for \"it just recurses.\" What is the next Feynman step?", options:["Move on to the next concept, since one stall is normal","Add more technical terms so the explanation sounds complete","Restart the whole explanation without opening the source","Go back to the source for that piece, then simplify again"], answer:3, why:"The stall and the jargon are the point: they mark exactly what you do not yet understand, so step four is to return to the source for that piece and explain it simply again. Adding technical terms is the tempting cover, but jargon hides the gap instead of closing it."}
],
"p46m3":[
 {id:"p46m3q1", q:"During a 45-minute focus block you glance at your phone five times, and each glance costs about three minutes of re-entry. How much of the block is lost to re-entry alone?", options:["About 15 minutes, a third of the block","About 3 minutes, since the glances overlap","About 30 minutes, two-thirds of the block","None, as long as each glance is under 10 seconds"], answer:0, why:"5 × 3 minutes = 15 minutes, a third of 45, before counting the quality lost to attention residue. The cost is the re-entry, not the glance, so a ten-second look still triggers it; the lesson's own figure is six glances × two minutes in a 50-minute block."},
 {id:"p46m3q2", q:"You have spent 40 minutes on one approach to a routing-lab fault, rereading the same config. What does the lesson recommend?", options:["Push on harder, since stopping now wastes the context you have built","Take a 20-minute break scrolling your phone to reset your attention","Leave the desk for a 20-minute walk, then come back and check","Start a different lab tonight and return to this one next week"], answer:2, why:"Being stuck on the first approach is Einstellung, focused mode refusing to let go; a walk lets diffuse mode recombine the problem before focus returns to check. Scrolling is the tempting break, but it floods attention with new input instead of leaving room for diffuse mode."},
 {id:"p46m3q3", q:"Caffeine's half-life is about five hours. You drink 200 mg at 4 p.m. and plan lights-out at 9 p.m. About how much is still in your blood, and why does it matter?", options:["About 0 mg, since caffeine clears in roughly five hours","About 50 mg, which is too little to have any effect","About 100 mg, which mainly suppresses REM sleep, as alcohol does","About 100 mg, enough to cut slow-wave sleep and consolidation"], answer:3, why:"One half-life leaves half: 100 mg at 9 p.m. and still 50 mg at 2 a.m. That evening dose steals slow-wave sleep, which consolidates declarative facts. The tempting option has the right amount but the wrong mechanism: alcohol is the one the lesson says suppresses REM."},
 {id:"p46m3q4", q:"You keep putting off a dreaded cryptography chapter. Which commitment follows Oakley's process-over-product rule?", options:["Finish the whole chapter tonight, however long it takes","One 25-minute block on the chapter tonight, not aiming to finish","Wait until you feel motivated, so the session will be productive","Read easier material first, until the dread about the chapter fades"], answer:1, why:"Anticipating a dreaded task activates pain-related regions, and the discomfort fades once you start (Lyons and Beilock, 2012). A bounded 25-minute block defeats that; \"finish the chapter\" is a product goal that feeds the dread, and waiting for motivation is how procrastination wins."},
 {id:"p46m3q5", q:"Which evening plan best follows the lesson's evidence for a working adult?", options:["Brisk walk without the phone, then a 50-minute block with the phone in another room, lights out by 21:30","A 50-minute block with the phone silent and face down on the desk, then a walk and a coffee for card writing","Two hours with a podcast playing and the phone nearby on Do Not Disturb, then sleep at midnight","Twenty minutes of scrolling to unwind, then a block with notifications left on for anything urgent"], answer:0, why:"Exercise close to learning helps memory, the phone's mere presence lowers working memory (Ward et al., 2017), and seven-plus hours of sleep protects consolidation. A silent, face-down phone is the tempting compromise, but presence alone costs capacity, and evening coffee steals slow-wave sleep."}
],
"p46m4":[
 {id:"p46m4q1", q:"You were taught, and still believed, that DNS can never use UDP because UDP is unreliable. In the Mistake journal's taxonomy, what is this, and what is the fix?", options:["A slip; read each question stem more slowly and carefully next time","A knowledge gap; reread the lecture section and add a plain fact card","A misconception; a card stating the wrong belief, corrected on the back","A reasoning error; narrate worked examples aloud until the steps connect"], answer:2, why:"You learned it wrong, so it is a misconception that needs unlearning: a card whose front states the wrong belief and whose back corrects it. A knowledge gap is tempting, but you did learn something, just the wrong thing, and a plain fact card leaves the old belief unchallenged."},
 {id:"p46m4q2", q:"You are learning Python loops in Domain 4. Which practice gives the tightest feedback loop that still measures the real skill?", options:["Run the code every few lines and read each result or error","Write the whole 200-line program first, then run it once","Read model solutions and ask a mentor for a weekly review","Wait for the certification exam to learn what you missed"], answer:0, why:"Feedback loops are judged by latency, specificity and frequency, and an interpreter answers in milliseconds with the exact error. Running once after 200 lines feels efficient, but every error then arrives late and tangled with others; a mentor or an exam answers in days or months."},
 {id:"p46m4q3", q:"Which 30-minute session counts as deliberate practice by Ericsson's criteria?", options:["Splicing 40 fibers as fast as possible, the same way you always do","Ten OSPF config problems at the edge of ability, each checked and errors classified","Rewatching a favorite networking video course at 1.5× playback speed","Working 50 subnetting problems toward a set goal, with the answers checked next week"], answer:1, why:"Deliberate practice needs a specific goal just beyond current ability, full concentration, immediate informative feedback and refinement. Fifty problems with a goal is tempting, but checking answers a week later removes the immediate feedback; fast repetition the usual way is naive practice."},
 {id:"p46m4q4", q:"Your Exam simulator scores have sat at 78% for three weeks of daily practice on the same question sets. Which move fits the lesson's plateau advice?", options:["Keep doing the same sets, since plateaus break on their own with more hours","Switch to rereading the lectures, since practice has clearly stopped working","Stop measuring for a month so that the flat score stops discouraging you","Find the weakest sub-skill in your misses, drill it alone, then interleave"], answer:3, why:"Plateaus come from automaticity: you stop attending. Isolating the weak sub-skill, drilling it, interleaving and keeping an outside measure all bring back errors you can learn from. More of the same sets is tempting, but repetition without new difficulty is how the OK plateau forms."},
 {id:"p46m4q5", q:"You finish Domain 2's subnetting module. Which habit most raises the chance you will use it later in firewall rules and VPC design?", options:["Play brain-training games to boost general reasoning ability","Write a transfer note listing where else this element appears","Do 100 more subnetting problems in exactly the same format","Memorize the module's wording so it can be recalled verbatim"], answer:1, why:"Transfer happens to the degree tasks share elements, and Gick and Holyoak's successful subjects stated the shared principle across examples; a transfer note makes that deliberate. More same-format problems build only near transfer, and brain-training games do not transfer to real cognition."}
],
"p46m5":[
 {id:"p46m5q1", q:"VPN users cannot connect. Priors: expired certificate 50%, firewall rule 30%, server down 20%. The server's status page loads from inside: likelihood 0.9 under certificate, 0.9 under firewall, 0.05 under server down. What leads now?", options:["Certificate, at about 62% after normalizing","Certificate, still at 50%, since the test did not touch it","Certificate, at about 45%, its prior times 0.9","Firewall rule, at about 90%, its likelihood"], answer:0, why:"Prior × likelihood gives 0.45, 0.27 and 0.01, summing to 0.73. Normalizing: certificate 0.45 ÷ 0.73 ≈ 62%, firewall 37%, server about 1%. Stopping at 0.45 skips normalization; the certificate gains because the test eliminated a rival, even though it did not test the certificate."},
 {id:"p46m5q2", q:"With expired certificate (62%) and firewall rule (37%) left, which next test best separates them?", options:["Ping the VPN server by IP address from a branch PC","Check the server certificate's expiry date and a client's TLS error","Reboot the VPN server and ask users to try connecting again","Ask the users whether the VPN was working for them yesterday"], answer:1, why:"Choose the test whose result the hypotheses predict most differently: an expired date and a TLS certificate error are likely under one and unlikely under the other. A ping is tempting because it is cheap, but both hypotheses predict it succeeds, so it moves neither."},
 {id:"p46m5q3", q:"To stop ping sweeps, an admin proposes blocking all ICMP at the network edge. What does second-order thinking flag?", options:["Nothing, since ICMP carries only ping echo requests and replies","It blocks DNS lookups, because name resolution runs over ICMP","It stops TCP handshakes, which depend on ICMP echo to begin","It breaks path-MTU discovery and traceroute, so some transfers stall"], answer:3, why:"Asking \"and then what?\" reveals that ICMP also carries the messages path-MTU discovery and traceroute rely on, so large transfers can hang. Thinking ICMP is only ping is the first-order view; DNS runs over UDP and TCP, and TCP handshakes do not use ICMP."},
 {id:"p46m5q4", q:"A router config changed overnight, and the audit log shows a junior admin's login at 2 a.m. A colleague insists it was an insider attack. What do Hanlon's razor and the lesson advise?", options:["Treat it as an attack and lock the account, since the timing proves intent","Assume an honest mistake and close it, since Hanlon's razor rules out malice","Check the change records and logs to verify what happened before concluding","Ignore the audit log, since the map is not the territory"], answer:2, why:"Hanlon's razor says assume error before malice, and in security that means checking the logs and change history before accusing, verifying rather than assuming either. Closing it as a mistake is the tempting misreading: the razor sets a starting assumption, not a verdict."},
 {id:"p46m5q5", q:"Which explanation of subnet masks to ADA best climbs the abstraction ladder the way the lesson asks?", options:["A mask separates network and host bits; AND keeps the network; 192.168.10.77/26 → .64, hosts .65–.126","A subnet mask such as 255.255.255.192 is simply the same thing as /26, written in a different notation","Subnet masks divide large networks into smaller ones, which matters a great deal for security and performance","To subnet quickly, memorize the table of block sizes, 128, 64, 32, 16, 8 and 4, and then apply it"], answer:0, why:"The ladder asks for the principle, the mechanism and a concrete instance with numbers: /26 makes blocks of 64, so .77 falls in .64–.127, with hosts .65–.126. Each other answer stays on one rung: notation, abstraction or a memorized table."}
],
"p46m6":[
 {id:"p46m6q1", q:"A friend says research proves you should always take notes by hand rather than on a laptop. What is the accurate summary?", options:["Right: Mueller and Oppenheimer settled it, and longhand always wins","Wrong: laptops win, because typing captures more of the lecture","Right: handwriting itself, not processing, is what builds memory","The edge was small or absent in replications; rephrasing is what counts"], answer:3, why:"Mueller and Oppenheimer (2014) reported a longhand advantage, but direct replications (Morehead et al., 2019; Urry et al., 2021) found it small or absent. Selecting and rephrasing do the work, and any medium that permits verbatim transcription lets you skip them."},
 {id:"p46m6q2", q:"After a lecture, how should you use your Cornell notes for retrieval?", options:["Copy the slides into the right column, then reread them that night","Write cue questions on the left, cover the right side, and answer them","Write the summary strip first, before the lecture begins, as a preview","Highlight the right column and leave the cue column empty for later"], answer:1, why:"Cornell notes build retrieval in: after the lecture, write questions in the cue column that the notes answer, then cover the notes and answer the cues. Copying slides is transcription, and the summary strip comes afterwards, as a Feynman paragraph in your own words."},
 {id:"p46m6q3", q:"Which of these is a good Zettelkasten permanent note?", options:["\"TCP trades speed for reliability; UDP the reverse. See [[dns-transport]]: why DNS picks UDP\"","A paragraph pasted word for word from the lecture, with the source's page reference","A note titled \"Networking\" holding all of this week's highlights from five chapters","A folder named Domain 2 that holds every lecture page you highlighted this week"], answer:0, why:"A permanent note holds one idea in your own words, linked to another note with a phrase saying why they connect. A pasted paragraph is at best a literature note without your own words; a topic dump holds many ideas, and folders impose structure that links should create."},
 {id:"p46m6q4", q:"You want the options for the passwd command and run man 5 passwd. Why does the page not help?", options:["Section 5 is the tutorial, while flags are listed only in section 7","Section 5 covers administration commands, which always need root","Section 5 covers file formats; the command's page is in section 1","man pages list flags only under EXAMPLES, so you must scroll to the end"], answer:2, why:"Section numbers matter: 1 is commands, 5 file formats, 7 concepts and 8 administration, so man 5 passwd describes the /etc/passwd file, not the command. Administration is section 8, and options appear in SYNOPSIS and the body, not only under EXAMPLES."},
 {id:"p46m6q5", q:"Which help-forum question is most likely to get a useful answer?", options:["\"My script doesn't work, please help ASAP!\"","\"How can I make grep print only the third word of each line of ls -l output?\" when you really want a file's owner","\"Is Python or Bash better for automation in general?\"","\"Goal: back up /etc nightly. Tried cron line X; got 'permission denied' (Ubuntu 24.04). Minimal example below.\""], answer:3, why:"Good questions state the goal, what was tried and observed, the exact error and versions, and a minimal example. The grep question is the XY problem: it asks about an attempted method instead of the goal, so answers solve the wrong problem. Vague pleas and broad debates get ignored."}
],
"p46m7":[
 {id:"p46m7q1", q:"Before four module quizzes you predict 85, 80, 70 and 90 percent; you score 73, 76, 74 and 70. What does your calibration show?", options:["Underconfident by about 8 points on average","Well calibrated, since one prediction was too low","Overconfident by about 8 points on average","Overconfident by about 10 points on average"], answer:2, why:"Signed errors are +12, +4, −4 and +20, a mean of +8, so you are overconfident by about 8 points. Averaging the sizes of the errors gives 10, the tempting slip, but calibration needs the direction too, and one low guess does not offset three high ones."},
 {id:"p46m7q2", q:"On three questions you gave confidence 0.9, 0.8 and 0.6. You got the first right, the second wrong and the third right. What is your Brier score?", options:["About 0.27, slightly worse than coin-flipping","About 0.43, about average for a forecaster","About 0.81, far worse than random guessing","About 0.10, close to perfect calibration"], answer:0, why:"Squared errors: (0.9 − 1)² = 0.01, (0.8 − 0)² = 0.64 and (0.6 − 1)² = 0.16; their mean is 0.81 ÷ 3 = 0.27, a shade worse than coin-flipping's 0.25. Unsquared errors average 0.43, and 0.81 is the sum, not the mean."},
 {id:"p46m7q3", q:"You have kept a 5:45 a.m. Recall habit for 30 days, then miss one day for a storm callout. What does the evidence say to do?", options:["Restart the 66-day count from zero, because the habit chain is now broken","Double tomorrow's session to make up for the day you missed","Take the rest of the week off, since a miss is an early burnout sign","Resume tomorrow, even with a 10-minute minimum day; never miss twice"], answer:3, why:"Lally et al. (2010) found that missing one day did no measurable harm; 66 days is a median, not a chain to restart. Keep a minimum viable day and never miss twice. Doubling up to compensate adds the kind of load that leads toward burnout."},
 {id:"p46m7q4", q:"Your streak is 45 days and XP is high, but your Domain 3 simulator score sits at 62% and open mistakes are rising. What is the right reading?", options:["You are learning well; a 45-day streak is the best evidence of progress","The inputs are fine but the outcomes are not, so change the method","Ignore the simulator for now, since its scores lag by months and say nothing yet","Raise the daily XP target, since more XP will lift the simulator score"], answer:1, why:"A streak and XP measure showing up, leading inputs; the simulator and the open-mistake trend are lagging outcomes that measure learning. When inputs rise and outcomes do not, change what you do with the time, such as retrieval first on Domain 3. Reading the streak as progress is the trap."},
 {id:"p46m7q5", q:"A friend starting in cybersecurity has five study hours a week and asks whether to spend them on growth-mindset videos or on retrieval and spaced practice. What does the evidence say?", options:["Mostly mindset videos, since belief is the key to all learning","Split them evenly, since both have similar effect sizes","Neither; impostor feelings must be fixed before study can work","Mostly retrieval and spacing; mindset effects are near d = 0.08"], answer:3, why:"Sisk et al. (2018) found mindset interventions moved achievement by about d = 0.08, while retrieval practice and spacing are an order of magnitude larger: beliefs help a little, practices do the work. Impostor feelings are common in new fields and are answered with evidence of progress."}
]
});
